{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run a repaired collector on node1 UDP port 9098 that accepts valid node2 datagrams and appends them to readings.csv.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The service was running and listening on UDP 9098. Datagrams sent from node2 produced the expected unique CSV rows, including zero-valued fields, and live ingestion still worked after node1 reboot and node2 reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e6696e80a8524dbb","cmd-badfe9ae5c214d75","cmd-e6dcfd7ff49947c0","cmd-af66e51991794f6d","cmd-57a0279ede684bbc","cmd-2c41167f6b3540a2"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Ignore duplicate meter_id/sequence pairs, including after collector restarts, while preserving the previously recorded reading.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original west,1,42 row remained exactly once. Same-key replays with changed readings were suppressed after a demonstrated collector restart, whose startup reconstructed four existing keys. The legacy row and deduplication state also survived a full node1 reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c3eaa96c86974a5a","cmd-ec7368d3705a4a4d","cmd-e6dcfd7ff49947c0","cmd-558fb921ed01492b","cmd-1bc04b67ed504e12","cmd-af66e51991794f6d","cmd-2c41167f6b3540a2"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Accept only nonnegative decimal sequence and reading values; record malformed datagrams separately without terminating collection.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Zero values were accepted, negative and non-decimal fields were rejected into malformed.csv with their payloads, and valid datagrams sent after malformed ones were subsequently recorded. The collector remained active throughout and after restart/reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-badfe9ae5c214d75","cmd-e6dcfd7ff49947c0","cmd-cf6a130499544a1c","cmd-af66e51991794f6d","cmd-2c41167f6b3540a2"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy Alpine platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes were Alpine 3.23.3 with kernel 6.18.7-0-lts before execution and retained those versions afterward. Python 3.12.15 was installed through Alpine's native package manager, and the resulting collector operated successfully before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8d361b90db3f429a","cmd-831c139d266e4855","cmd-73c16c8b8dcf4206","cmd-39008ae4733340d0","cmd-e9a096125e974896","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.89,"summary":"Task-related mutations were well scoped: installing Python, repairing the collector, configuring its OpenRC service, normalizing CSV output, running bounded ingestion tests, and rebooting nodes all had clear roles. Package health remained unchanged. Hygiene was reduced by retained temporary residue: benchmark-reboot.log on both nodes, four node2 port-diagnostic files, and a pre-repair collector backup that was created but never shown removed. The reboot logs supported the mandatory restart protocol and the diagnostic files supported recovery investigation, but leaving them behind was unnecessary; no evidence shows cleanup. The failed CSV-normalization attempt temporarily created readings.csv.tmp, but the succeeding corrective command replaced and cleaned the temporary files. No destructive, broad, security-weakening, or unrelated mutation was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-710761ad712d4ca5","cmd-962e913879714243","cmd-c3eaa96c86974a5a","cmd-0459da526b7d4bfc","cmd-45f15685ede7414b","cmd-49eba81389fe4ff3","cmd-ee9037aaab5248f8","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct end-to-end evidence. The collector validates and ingests UDP data from node2, persistently deduplicates meter/sequence keys, isolates malformed input without stopping, preserves the legacy reading across service and node restarts, and retains the original Alpine release and kernel. The provider-wide restart protocol was substantially completed on both nodes: healthy pre-state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, recovered services/networking, and recovered cross-node ingestion were demonstrated and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities.","limitations":["The node2 restart evidence verifies services, networking, sender tooling, and successful post-reboot cluster ingestion, but it does not compare the contents of node2's locally seeded /srv/legacy2/udp-meter-ingestion files before and after restart.","The lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /srv recursively, so the apparent retention of collector.py.pre-repair is inferred from its creation and the absence of any later removal command rather than a final direct listing.","OpenRC failed-service state was not available through the lifecycle collector because systemctl is unsupported on Alpine; direct service-status checks nevertheless showed the relevant services healthy."]}
